entry.summary:
The problem I was scratching
Every time I was debugging a web app, the same friction kept happening: I'd So I built Network Sniper, a Chrome extension that puts an editable, It's local-first by design: no telemetry, no cloud sync, sensitive headers I posted a Show HN. It didn't hit the front page — under 10 upvotes, a The bigger lesson came from what happened after launch, not the launch To support resending requests to any API the user is debugging (which by any origin — that's the whole point of the tool), my "host_permissions": ["*://*/*"]
This works, technically. It also means Chrome shows every installer a "Read and change all your data on all websites." For a The fix was to stop asking for broad access up front, and instead request "optional_host_permissions": ["*://*/*"]
// only requested at the moment of use, scoped to the exact origin const granted = await chrome.permissions.request({ origins: [targetOriginPattern], });
Approved origins get cached for the session so the user isn't re-prompted capability, that doesn't mean it needs broad permission — request narrowly, and only when the user's action actually This one stung more. A few days after launch, I was checking install I did a full audit: grep'd the entire codebase and build output for any The likely explanation: Chrome Web Store's own Developer Dashboard lets store views — a completely separate thing from the extension's I still added a regression test that scans the entire source tree and don't add . Not because I found a violation, but because "I was pretty Network Sniper is live, permission-minimal, and — as far as a real, If you debug APIs and have felt the Postman-copy-paste friction, I'd It's free, Manifest V3, and — now provably — doesn't phone home.
